seoDescription: Why I built a content workflow with a separate voice check, stress test and verified delivery path.
---

The first draft sounded acceptable.

The second sounded like every loud marketing post I avoid.

Both came from the same writing instructions.

This is the problem behind my Content Repurposer. Producing more text was never the hard part. Keeping the voice intact after the source, format and model changed was harder.

## I learned early how instructions drift in use

As a back-up trainer, I worked with new hires and seasonal agents. I wrote process documents, built training material and reviewed how people applied it under live conditions.

A good slide deck did not guarantee a good call.

The written process mattered. The review after the work mattered too.

I later worked in brand-facing roles and ran a personal training practice. Both made inconsistency visible. A message, routine or programme loses trust when the experience changes each time someone touches it.

When I moved into AI systems, I recognised the same issue in prompts.

An instruction is a starting point. It is not proof of the final behaviour.

## I built the checker after the writer

The Content Repurposer takes one finished source and writes three pieces: a LinkedIn post, a five-slide carousel and a 30-second video script.

Then a separate code step checks the result.

The LinkedIn draft needs to stay within its word range and end on a statement. The carousel needs exactly five slides. A list of phrases catches language I do not publish.

Anything failing the check enters a rewrite queue.

The rejected draft stays in the database. I still want to see what went wrong.

## The stress test mattered more than the clean run

The first live run used a real note from one of my builds. The three outputs passed.

For the second run, I fed the workflow source material packed with empty superlatives, unsupported claims and manufactured urgency.

The model resisted most of it.

Two prohibited phrases survived. The LinkedIn post also stopped at 57 words, below the required range.

The code caught every failure and withheld the package from the delivery route.

Two runs averaged 7.08 seconds for all three formats.

## A pass needed to reach the outside world

I later installed the repository from scratch on a clean n8n instance.

The workflow had to load, accept a real test message, create the records and deliver the Telegram alert. A real message ID became the pass condition.

This caught the kind of problems a creator’s existing setup hides: stale instructions, missing fields, local assumptions and steps named differently from the form sending data.

The test treated documentation as part of the product.

My training background would not let me call a process complete if only its author knew how to run it.

## The real product is a repeatable standard

The workflow saves time, but speed is not the reason I built it.

I built it because voice is easy to describe and hard to protect across repeated output.

The writer proposes. The checker compares the draft against a visible standard. I make the final decision.

The split keeps AI in the role I trust: producing options quickly while leaving taste and accountability with the person publishing the work.

Built by handlit.
